 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Prohibits the establishment of new for-profit hospices or increasing the capacity of existing for-profit hospices.",
 "title": "Relates to prohibiting the establishment of new for-profit hospices",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A565",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A565-2025",
     "printNo": "A565",
     "session": 2025,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 118\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 565, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend the public health law, in relation to the estab-\n      lishment, incorporation, construction, or increase in capacity  of\n      for-profit hospice\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would prohibit the establishment of for-profit hospices or\nexpansion of existing for-profit hospices in New York.\n \n  Hospice and palliative care services provide essential care for  aging\nindividuals  and  those  with  disabilities.  While for-profit hospices,\noften backed by private equity or venture capital, have rapidly expanded\nand tend to provide a narrower range and lower quality  of  services  to\npatients  than  not-for-profit hospices, in New York, there are only two\nfor-profit hospice providers. Moreover, the Department of Health  has  a\nrigorous  certificate  of need process and provides ongoing supervision,\nwhich provides adequate guardrails and protections for hospice patients.\nThe Department also established the Center for  Hospice  and  Palliative\nCare  in  2024  to  ensure New York is well-positioned to ensure quality\nend-of-life choices.\n \n  In fact, data suggests that hospice care is underutilized in New  York\ncompared to other states, straining other elements of New York's health-\ncare  system,  a  concern that the bill could exacerbate. To ensure that\nresidents of New York have adequate access  to  hospice  and  palliative\ncare,  I am directing the Department to update and modernize its method-\nology for determining need and pursue efforts to increase awareness  and\nexpand the use of hospice care in appropriate settings.\n\n  Therefore, I am constrained to veto the bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
    "vetoNumber": 118,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2025
   }
